('127.257.d.5.G.ii',3,'To cope with uncertainty in a cash budget, a firm can build in:','["A single exact guess", "Zero cash", "A safety cushion or scenario analysis", "No assumptions"]'::jsonb,2,'Buffers and scenarios handle uncertainty.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.G.ii',4,'Preparing best-case, expected, and worst-case cash budgets is called:','["Closing entries", "Depreciation", "Bank reconciliation", "Scenario or sensitivity analysis"]'::jsonb,3,'Scenarios test assumptions.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.G.iii',3,'Beginning cash is $5,000, receipts are $40,000, and disbursements are $38,000. Ending cash is:','["$7,000", "$2,000", "$3,000", "$83,000"]'::jsonb,0,'5,000 + 40,000 - 38,000 = 7,000.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.G.iii',4,'If the firm wants a minimum cash balance of $10,000, it must borrow:','["$7,000", "$3,000", "$17,000", "$0"]'::jsonb,1,'10,000 - 7,000 = 3,000.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.G.iv',3,'If a cash budget shows a deficit in March, management could respond by:','["Ignoring March", "Spending more", "Arranging a short-term loan", "Closing the bank account"]'::jsonb,2,'Deficits require funding.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.G.iv',4,'A cash budget that predicts a large surplus suggests the firm should:','["Leave it idle forever", "Hide it", "Spend it all", "Invest the extra cash in short-term securities"]'::jsonb,3,'Idle cash earns nothing.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.H',3,'A pro forma income statement is a:','["Projected statement based on forecasts", "Payroll record", "Tax return", "Historical statement"]'::jsonb,0,'Pro forma means projected.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.H',4,'Sales are $500,000 now and expected to rise 10%. If cost of goods sold stays at 60% of sales, projected cost of goods sold is:','["$33,000", "$330,000", "$550,000", "$300,000"]'::jsonb,1,'550,000 x 0.60 = 330,000.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.I',3,'Short-term financial management focuses mainly on:','["Long-term bonds", "Retirement plans", "Current assets and current liabilities", "Factory construction"]'::jsonb,2,'It manages working capital.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.I',4,'If inventory days are 45, receivable days 30, and payable days 25, the cash conversion cycle is:','["40 days", "100 days", "20 days", "50 days"]'::jsonb,3,'45 + 30 - 25 = 50.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.J.i',3,'Valuing a proposed project helps decide whether to:','["Commit capital to it", "Redecorate", "Change office hours", "Hire a new mascot"]'::jsonb,0,'Valuation guides allocation.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.J.i',4,'When capital is limited, the firm should fund projects in order of:','["Alphabetical order", "Highest value created per dollar invested", "Newest first", "Lowest cost first"]'::jsonb,1,'Capital rationing favors efficient projects.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.J.ii',3,'Flexibility, such as the option to expand a project later, can be valued using:','["A weather forecast", "Payroll", "Real options analysis", "A mission statement"]'::jsonb,2,'Real options value flexibility.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.J.ii',4,'The option to abandon a failing project for its salvage value makes the project:','["Less valuable", "Illegal", "Worthless", "More valuable than a rigid version"]'::jsonb,3,'Options add value.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.J.iii',3,'A company worth more than the book value of its assets shows that investors expect:','["Future earnings beyond current assets", "No profits", "Immediate bankruptcy", "No growth"]'::jsonb,0,'Market value reflects expectations.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.J.iii',4,'Valuation is a part of business finance because decisions to buy, sell, or invest depend on:','["Logos", "Estimated value versus price", "Office size", "Slogans"]'::jsonb,1,'Value drives deals.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.i',3,'A firm choosing between a bond issue and a stock issue compares the cost and:','["Dates of holidays", "Names of the printers", "Risk and control effects of each", "Colors of the certificates"]'::jsonb,2,'Financing choices trade off cost and risk.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.i',4,'Issuing new common stock to fund expansion tends to:','["Lower the share count", "Raise debt", "Remove all risk", "Dilute existing owners'' control"]'::jsonb,3,'More shares spread ownership.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.ii',3,'A corporate bond is a:','["Loan from investors that pays interest", "Insurance policy", "Share of ownership", "Tax form"]'::jsonb,0,'Bondholders are lenders.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.ii',4,'A $1,000 bond with a 7% coupon paid semiannually pays each period:','["$70", "$35", "$7", "$3.50"]'::jsonb,1,'70/2 = 35.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.iii',3,'A firm borrows at 8% and its tax rate is 25%. The after-tax cost of debt is:','["8%", "10%", "6%", "2%"]'::jsonb,2,'8 x 0.75 = 6.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.iii',4,'Interest on debt is tax-deductible, so after-tax cost of debt is:','["Zero always", "Equal to the tax rate", "Higher than the rate", "Lower than the stated interest rate"]'::jsonb,3,'The tax shield reduces the cost.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.iv',3,'When a corporation issues stock, it receives cash and the investor receives:','["An ownership share", "An insurance policy", "A loan note", "A tax credit"]'::jsonb,0,'Stock is equity.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.iv',4,'An initial public offering is the first time a company:','["Hires employees", "Sells stock to the general public", "Pays taxes", "Opens a bank account"]'::jsonb,1,'IPO means going public.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.v',3,'Compared with common stock, preferred stock usually has:','["Voting rights", "No dividend", "Priority for dividends but no voting rights", "Lower claim on assets"]'::jsonb,2,'Preferred has dividend priority.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.v',4,'Common stockholders differ from preferred holders in that common holders:','["Cannot vote", "Are paid first always", "Have fixed dividends", "Receive residual earnings after other claims"]'::jsonb,3,'Common is the residual claim.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.vi',3,'A preferred stock pays a $6 dividend and sells for $80. Its cost is:','["7.5%", "80%", "13.3%", "6%"]'::jsonb,0,'6/80 = 0.075.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.vi',4,'A common stock expects a $2 dividend next year, trades at $40, and dividends grow 4% yearly. Its cost is:','["5%", "9%", "4%", "8%"]'::jsonb,1,'2/40 + 4% = 9%.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.vii',3,'A firm with debt of $400,000 and equity of $200,000 has a debt-to-equity ratio of:','["0.5", "1.0", "2.0", "3.0"]'::jsonb,2,'400,000 / 200,000 = 2.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.K.vii',4,'Using more debt (financial leverage) can magnify returns to owners but also increases:','["Safety", "Equity", "Liquidity ratio", "Risk of financial distress"]'::jsonb,3,'Leverage raises risk.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.L',3,'A distribution of cash to shareholders from earnings is a:','["Cash dividend", "Wage", "Stock split", "Bond coupon"]'::jsonb,0,'Dividends reward owners.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.L',4,'A 10% stock dividend on 1,000 shares gives holders of 1,000 shares:','["1,000 additional shares", "100 additional shares", "No shares", "10 additional shares"]'::jsonb,1,'10% of 1,000 = 100.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.M',3,'A firm that pays out a large share of earnings as dividends will more likely need:','["A lower stock price", "No funding", "External financing for growth", "Fewer investors"]'::jsonb,2,'Less retained cash means outside funding.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.M',4,'A growing company that retains more earnings generally has:','["No shareholders", "No profits", "Larger external needs", "Smaller external financing needs"]'::jsonb,3,'Retention funds growth internally.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.N',3,'Under the residual theory of dividends, a firm first funds:','["All acceptable investment projects", "Stock repurchases", "Officer bonuses", "Dividends"]'::jsonb,0,'Dividends come from what is left.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.N',4,'A firm earns $150,000 and needs $120,000 of equity for projects. Under the residual theory, dividends are:','["$150,000", "$30,000", "$270,000", "$120,000"]'::jsonb,1,'150,000 - 120,000 = 30,000.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.O',3,'According to the dividend irrelevance theory, firm value depends mainly on:','["Stock color", "Share certificates", "Investment earnings power rather than payout ratio", "Dividend size"]'::jsonb,2,'Modigliani-Miller argues payout does not matter.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.O',4,'Investors who prefer current income may favor firms that:','["Issue no stock", "Never pay dividends", "Cut dividends often", "Pay regular dividends"]'::jsonb,3,'Clientele effect.','original'),
