('127.257.d.5.P',4,'A stable dividend policy usually:','["Changes every quarter", "Pays a steady amount that signals confidence", "Ignores earnings", "Eliminates dividends"]'::jsonb,1,'Stability signals strength.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.Q',3,'A firm that wants to return cash to owners while giving them flexibility on taxes might choose a:','["Bond default", "Name change", "Stock repurchase", "New logo"]'::jsonb,2,'Buybacks defer taxation.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.Q',4,'A stock dividend differs from a cash dividend because it:','["Cancels the stock", "Lowers shares", "Pays cash", "Gives more shares instead of cash"]'::jsonb,3,'Stock dividends issue shares.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.R.i',3,'In a merger, two companies:','["Combine into one", "Compete forever", "Split apart", "Cancel contracts"]'::jsonb,0,'Mergers unite firms.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.R.i',4,'An acquisition differs from a merger because in an acquisition one company:','["Disappears with no payment", "Buys another and often keeps it as a subsidiary", "Is created from scratch", "Becomes a charity"]'::jsonb,1,'The buyer takes control.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.R.ii',3,'A hostile takeover occurs when a bidder:','["Borrows from a bank", "Buys inventory", "Pursues control without the target management''s approval", "Is invited by the target"]'::jsonb,2,'Management opposes the bid.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.R.ii',4,'A poison pill is a defense that makes a hostile takeover:','["Cheaper", "Faster", "Tax-free", "More expensive for the bidder"]'::jsonb,3,'Pills dilute the bidder''s stake.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.R.iii',3,'Employees often worry after a merger about:','["Job cuts and culture clashes", "Winning prizes", "New logos only", "Lower rent"]'::jsonb,0,'Integration affects people.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.R.iii',4,'One common merger problem is overpaying, which can result in:','["Lower taxes", "Losses that destroy shareholder value", "Guaranteed profits", "Higher dividends always"]'::jsonb,1,'Overpayment hurts returns.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.R.iv',3,'One way to value a target is to compare its price-to-earnings ratio to:','["Its landlord", "Its mascot", "Similar companies", "Its vendors'' pets"]'::jsonb,2,'Comparables set a benchmark.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.R.iv',4,'Estimating the present value of a target''s expected future cash flows is called:','["Petty cash", "Trial balancing", "Closing", "Discounted cash flow valuation"]'::jsonb,3,'DCF values cash flows.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.R.v',3,'A target is attractive when it has strong cash flow and the purchase price is:','["Below its estimated value", "Above its estimated value", "Unknown", "Zero"]'::jsonb,0,'Buy below value.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.R.v',4,'A buyer values a target at $50 million and learns hidden liabilities of $8 million. The adjusted value is:','["$58 million", "$42 million", "$50 million", "$8 million"]'::jsonb,1,'50 - 8 = 42.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.R.vi',3,'Selling a division to focus on core businesses is a:','["IPO", "Merger", "Divestiture", "Dividend"]'::jsonb,2,'Divestitures shrink the firm.','original'),
('127.257.d.5.R.vi',4,'Creating a separate public company from a division and giving its shares to current owners is a:','["Audit", "Stock split", "Bond issue", "Spin-off"]'::jsonb,3,'Spin-offs separate units.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.A.i',3,'After accounting scandals, the Sarbanes-Oxley Act strengthened:','["Auditor independence and executive accountability", "Retail pricing", "Zoning rules", "School funding"]'::jsonb,0,'SOX targeted fraud.','original')
