('127.257.d.6.A.i',4,'The Public Company Accounting Oversight Board, created by Sarbanes-Oxley, oversees:','["Restaurants", "Auditors of public companies", "Car dealers", "Banks'' tellers"]'::jsonb,1,'PCAOB regulates audit firms.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.A.ii',3,'The SEC regulates accounting for public companies by requiring:','["No audits", "Secret books", "Audited filings that follow set standards", "Handwritten ledgers"]'::jsonb,2,'The SEC enforces disclosure.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.A.ii',4,'The SEC has authority over accounting standards but has historically delegated their setting to the:','["Federal Reserve", "IRS", "Texas legislature", "Financial Accounting Standards Board"]'::jsonb,3,'FASB sets GAAP.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.A.iii',3,'State regulation of accounting includes:','["Licensing CPAs and disciplining misconduct", "Printing tax forms", "Banning audits", "Setting stock prices"]'::jsonb,0,'States license accountants.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.A.iii',4,'To become a CPA in Texas, a candidate must meet education, pass the exam, and satisfy:','["Retirement age", "Experience and ethics requirements", "A cooking test", "Residency in a dorm"]'::jsonb,1,'Licensing has several parts.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.A.iv',3,'A key difference between IFRS and U.S. GAAP is that IFRS is generally more:','["Optional", "Secret", "Principles-based", "Rules-based"]'::jsonb,2,'IFRS gives broad principles.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.A.iv',4,'Under IFRS, the inventory method that is not allowed is:','["Weighted average", "Specific identification", "FIFO", "LIFO"]'::jsonb,3,'IFRS prohibits LIFO.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.B.i',3,'An accountant investigating possible fraud starts by writing:','["Questions about what happened and how it was hidden", "Press releases", "Tax returns", "Sales slogans"]'::jsonb,0,'Questions guide inquiry.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.B.i',4,'Which question best guides a case study of a revenue recognition scandal?','["What color is the logo?", "Which transactions were recorded early and why?", "Who is the oldest worker?", "How big is the lobby?"]'::jsonb,1,'Focus on the accounting issue.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.B.ii',3,'Relevant sources for a fraud case study include:','["Rumors", "Memes", "Court filings and SEC enforcement releases", "Fan posts"]'::jsonb,2,'Official records are key.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.B.ii',4,'Evidence gathered in a fraud case should be:','["Memorized only", "Unlabeled", "Verbal only", "Documented and traceable to its source"]'::jsonb,3,'Documentation supports credibility.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.B.iii',3,'A source is more valid and reliable when it is:','["Written by an expert and supported by evidence", "Written to sell something", "Unsourced", "Anonymous"]'::jsonb,0,'Expertise and evidence matter.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.B.iii',4,'A researcher finds two accounts of a company''s restatement that conflict. The best step is to:','["Trust the funnier one", "Compare them with the company''s SEC filing", "Choose the shorter one", "Ignore both"]'::jsonb,1,'Primary filings resolve conflicts.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.B.iv',3,'After describing a fraud, the analyst should identify which:','["Songs are popular", "Foods were served", "Laws and regulations were violated or apply", "Colors were used"]'::jsonb,2,'Link facts to law.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.B.iv',4,'Falsely reporting revenue to investors could violate:','["Parking ordinances", "Jaywalking rules", "Zoning laws", "Securities fraud provisions"]'::jsonb,3,'Misstatements break securities laws.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.B.v',3,'Evidence of fraud in the books may include:','["Unusual journal entries made near period end", "Blank pages", "Neat handwriting", "Regular schedules"]'::jsonb,0,'Odd entries are red flags.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.B.v',4,'A pattern of receivables rising much faster than sales may indicate:','["Lower taxes", "Possible revenue overstatement", "Strong collections", "Greater cash"]'::jsonb,1,'It can signal fictitious sales.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.B.vi',3,'A clear case study presentation should include correct grammar, spelling, and:','["Unrelated art", "No conclusion", "Citations of the sources used", "Random facts"]'::jsonb,2,'Citations credit sources.','original'),
('127.257.d.6.B.vi',4,'Citing sources in a presentation helps the audience:','["Remove facts", "Skip slides", "Avoid reading", "Check the evidence themselves"]'::jsonb,3,'Transparency builds trust.','original'),
('127.257.d.7.A',3,'An auditor uses data mining software to find:','["Duplicate payments and unusual patterns", "New logos", "Weather data", "New customers"]'::jsonb,0,'Software searches big data sets.','original'),
('127.257.d.7.A',4,'Running an analysis on 100% of transactions instead of a sample improves the chance of:','["Raising errors", "Detecting anomalies", "Losing records", "Missing errors"]'::jsonb,1,'Full-population testing finds more issues.','original'),
('127.257.d.7.B',3,'A presentation of accounting results to nonaccountants should use:','["Unlabeled charts", "Only jargon", "Clear language and supporting visuals", "No data"]'::jsonb,2,'Clarity helps the audience.','original'),
('127.257.d.7.B',4,'A written report on a ratio analysis should state the findings and:','["Jokes", "Personal stories", "Unrelated facts", "Recommendations based on them"]'::jsonb,3,'Reports end with action.','original'),
('127.258.d.2',3,'A budget is a tool used to:','["Plan and control how money is used", "Elect officers", "Set interest rates", "Print currency"]'::jsonb,0,'Budgets plan resources.','original'),
('127.258.d.2',4,'Comparing actual spending to the budget each month is a way to:','["Cancel projects", "Monitor and control financial resources", "Raise taxes", "Avoid records"]'::jsonb,1,'Monitoring enables control.','original'),
('127.258.d.3',3,'A table of monthly sales for a year can be turned into a line graph to show:','["Logos", "Tax law", "Trends over time", "Employee names"]'::jsonb,2,'Graphs show patterns.','original'),
('127.258.d.3',4,'Monthly expenses of $4,000, $4,200, $3,800, and $4,000 have an average of:','["$4,200", "$16,000", "$3,800", "$4,000"]'::jsonb,3,'16,000 / 4 = 4,000.','original'),
('127.258.d.1.A',3,'The scope of accounting includes recording, classifying, summarizing, and:','["Interpreting financial information", "Selling products", "Advertising", "Hiring"]'::jsonb,0,'Accounting covers the whole process.','original'),
('127.258.d.1.A',4,'Which branch of accounting prepares reports for outside users such as investors?','["Personal budgeting", "Financial accounting", "Cost accounting only", "Managerial accounting"]'::jsonb,1,'Financial accounting serves outsiders.','original'),
('127.258.d.1.B',3,'Tracking money flow means recording where cash comes from and:','["What time it is", "Who works late", "Where it goes", "Which songs play"]'::jsonb,2,'Cash flow is inflow and outflow.','original'),
('127.258.d.1.B',4,'A business collects $90,000 and pays $75,000 in a month. Net cash flow is:','["-$15,000", "+$90,000", "+$165,000", "+$15,000"]'::jsonb,3,'90,000 - 75,000 = 15,000.','original'),
('127.258.d.4.A',3,'A budget is mainly a:','["Plan of expected revenue and expenses", "Loan note", "Tax form", "Record of past errors"]'::jsonb,0,'Budgets plan ahead.','original'),
('127.258.d.4.A',4,'A zero-based budget requires managers to:','["Spend nothing", "Justify every expense from scratch each period", "Copy last year", "Ignore goals"]'::jsonb,1,'Zero-based starts at zero.','original'),
('127.258.d.4.B',3,'Marginal revenue is the:','["Total of all revenue", "Average price", "Added revenue from selling one more unit", "Cost of one unit"]'::jsonb,2,'Marginal means additional.','original'),
('127.258.d.4.B',4,'A firm maximizes profit where marginal revenue equals:','["Price", "Total cost", "Fixed cost", "Marginal cost"]'::jsonb,3,'MR = MC is the rule.','original'),
('127.258.d.4.C',3,'Net income of $20,000 on sales of $200,000 gives a net profit margin of:','["10%", "100%", "1%", "20%"]'::jsonb,0,'20,000/200,000 = 0.10.','original'),
('127.258.d.4.C',4,'Current assets of $60,000 and current liabilities of $30,000 give a current ratio of:','["1.5", "2.0", "0.5", "3.0"]'::jsonb,1,'60,000/30,000 = 2.','original'),
('127.258.d.4.D',3,'To judge a company''s solvency, a reader would look mostly at the:','["Office layout", "Mission statement", "Balance sheet", "Logo"]'::jsonb,2,'The balance sheet lists assets and debts.','original'),
('127.258.d.4.D',4,'A rising net income but falling operating cash flow may indicate:','["No issue", "Higher liquidity", "Strong health", "Earnings quality problems"]'::jsonb,3,'Profit without cash is a warning.','original'),
('127.258.d.4.E',3,'Comparing each line of an income statement to a percentage of sales is called:','["Vertical (common-size) analysis", "Horizontal analysis", "Break-even analysis", "Trial balance"]'::jsonb,0,'Common-size compares to a base.','original'),
('127.258.d.4.E',4,'Comparing a company''s results across several years to see changes is:','["Bank reconciliation", "Trend (horizontal) analysis", "Ratio rounding", "Vertical analysis"]'::jsonb,1,'Horizontal analysis tracks change.','original'),
